# NOTES
# Structural finding 1 (confirms and generalizes the seed bug): RDM's own German Wikipedia article
# (de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rotterdamsche_Droogdok_Maatschappij, citing a Nov 2012 Spiegel piece) gives what
# reads as a curated, fairly complete list of the ~21 RPVs RDM actually built (1970s-80s), across NL, DE,
# ES, SE, CH, US. Of German plants, ONLY Brunsbüttel and Philippsburg 1 appear. This means the blanket
# "Rotterdam Dockyard (RDM)" assignment is very likely wrong for every OTHER KWU-line German plant in this
# batch (Biblis A/B, Brokdorf, Emsland, Grafenrheinfeld, Grohnde, Krümmel, Lingen, Mülheim-Kärlich,
# Neckarwestheim 1/2, Obrigheim, Stade, Unterweser, Vak Kahl, Würgassen) — confirmed as WRONG or flagged
# UNVERIFIABLE above, but the true replacement manufacturer (GHH, Klöckner, MAN, or other) could not be
# sourced for any of them this session. Recommend a dedicated follow-up pass targeting German-language
# metallurgy/engineering-history sources (e.g. GHH company histories) specifically for these RPVs.
# RDM IS correctly confirmed for: Brunsbüttel, Philippsburg 1, Borssele, and Dodewaard.
# RDM is CONFIRMED WRONG (known-bug case) for: Isar 2 (true manufacturer = Gutehoffnungshütte/GHH).
# New instance of the same bug NOT previously flagged: Dodewaard's rpv_manufacturer was listed as
# "General Electric" but RDM's own page and Dutch Wikipedia both credit RDM with Dodewaard's vessel —
# GE supplied the reactor design/license, not the pressure vessel itself.
#
# Structural finding 2 (KWU anachronism, distinct from the RDM bug): Kraftwerk Union (KWU) was formed in
# 1969 from the AEG + Siemens nuclear merger. Several plants in this batch began construction, or were
# fully built and operating, before 1969 — the DB's blanket "Kraftwerk Union (KWU)" constructor/
# architect_engineer/turbine_supplier attribution is chronologically impossible for these and is WRONG:
# Vak Kahl (built 1958-1961, actual builder AEG, HIGH confidence), Würgassen (construction began Jan
# 1968, actual builder AEG), Obrigheim (Siemens contract signed March 1965, turbine by BBC Mannheim
# contracted 1961), Stade (Siemens turnkey contract October 1967), Lingen (construction began 1964,
# likely AEG), Gundremmingen A (construction began Dec 1962), and HDR Großwelzheim (shut down 1971 after
# a short run, so construction/commissioning very likely predates 1969). For most of these the exact
# replacement value (AEG vs a specific consortium name) could only be inferred from chronology, not
# directly cited, and is marked WRONG with LOW-MED confidence rather than blessed as a firm fact.
#
# Structural finding 3: THTR-300's rpv_manufacturer field is a category error — THTR-300 used an
# integrated prestressed-concrete pressure vessel (Spannbetonbehälter), not a discrete steel RPV
# fabricated by a named company. Krupp also withdrew from the build consortium five days before the June
# 1971 groundbreaking; the actual consortium was Brown, Boveri & Cie (BBC) + Nukem. Similarly,
# Niederaichbach is a CO2-cooled, heavy-water pressure-TUBE reactor that may not have a conventional
# single RPV at all — flag both for schema/field-applicability review, not just data-value correction.
#
# Structural finding 4: possible unrelated data error surfaced incidentally — IAEA PRIS lists Greifswald
# unit 5's reactor model as VVER V-213, not V-230 as given in this batch's `model` column (units 3-4 are
# correctly V-230). Not one of the four fields in scope for this verification, but worth a separate check
# since it also affects which suppliers (e.g. Škoda's documented V-213 product line) are plausible for
# unit 5 specifically.
